void construct_path(char *name, int lenmax, off_t size, off_t pos, off_t *ppos) {
	off_t block;
	off_t divisor;
	int levels;
	int used;
	int l;

	assert(pos >= 0 && pos < size);
	block = pos / TREEPAGESIZE;
	*ppos = pos % TREEPAGESIZE;

	levels = tree_levels(size);
	divisor = 1;
	for (l = 0; l < levels; l++)
		divisor *= TREEDIRSIZE;

	used = strlen(name);
	for (l = levels; l >= 1; l--) {
		used += snprintf(name + used, lenmax - used, "/TREE%04X",
				 (unsigned int)(block / divisor));
		block %= divisor;
		divisor /= TREEDIRSIZE;
	}
	used += snprintf(name + used, lenmax - used, "/FILE%04X",
			 (unsigned int)block);
	assert(used < lenmax);
}
```

Hmm, wait, assert(used < lenmax) — the return value of snprintf can exceed the remaining space even if we've truncated. If lenmax-used becomes ≤ 0, snprintf is... passing a negative size to snprintf is UB. Guard: assert(used < lenmax) at each step? Let me restructure with a check:

