Wait, there's one problem: after `count_assert(buf[0] == 'A');` — the file is TREEPAGESIZE bytes, and we wrote the full page at offset 0 of the file... no wait, that's wrong! open_treefile returns the fd of the block file, and we wrote `buf` (4096 bytes) at the fd's current offset 0 — but the export offset is (TREEDIRSIZE+2)*TREEPAGESIZE + 5, so within the file the data should go at offset 5! In the actual server, we pwrite with foffset = pos % TREEPAGESIZE. In my unit test I just did write(fd, buf, 4096) at file offset 0 → st_size == 4096 ✓ (since I wrote 4096 bytes at offset 0, it still passes). And then on read-back, lseek(fd, ppos=5) and read 4091 bytes — data exists at 5..4095 (since it was written at 0) → buf[0]=='A' ✓. It works, but semantically it's sloppy. Better to pwrite at ppos, like the server does. Let's fix it: get ppos first via construct_path, then pwrite(fd, buf, TREEPAGESIZE - ppos, ppos)... more simply: keep writing the full 4096 at offset 0? In that case the st_size check is 4096 ✓, and the read at ppos=5 gets 'A' ✓. Actually this is fine as a pure unit test of the path/open functions — the fd is just an fd, and it's the server's job to position it. But then the "data can be read back" comment is a bit off. Let's make it correct anyway: pwrite at ppos.

Fix:
```c
	memset(buf, 'A', sizeof buf);
	fd = open_treefile(root, O_RDWR | O_CREAT, size,
			(TREEDIRSIZE + 2) * TREEPAGESIZE + 5, &mutex);
	count_assert(fd >= 0);
	/* the caller of open_treefile computes the in-file offset, and writes at that position */
	count_assert(pwrite(fd, buf, TREEPAGESIZE - 5, 5) == TREEPAGESIZE - 5);
	close(fd);
	...
	count_assert(st.st_size == TREEPAGESIZE - 5);
	count_assert(ppos == 5);
	read back: pread(fd, buf, TREEPAGESIZE - 5, ppos) == TREEPAGESIZE - 5; buf[0]=='A'
```

